    "intro": "St. Michael’s Anglican Church is a historic parish on Tower Street, serving the Rae Town and Hannah Town communities in central Kingston. Its origins date back to at least the 1830s, when it ministered to a diverse population, including many of African descent, during the final years of slavery. The original church was destroyed in the 1907 earthquake but was rebuilt, later expanding with a school and the famous Ormsby Hall. Today, St. Michael’s remains a vital institution, providing worship, education, and community support to the surrounding inner-city neighborhoods.",
    "history": "St. Michael’s was established by the early 1800s in the growing waterfront settlement of Rae Town, a community of fishermen, laborers, and tradespeople. Parish records confirm a 'St. Michael’s Chapel' existed by 1834, operating during the era of slavery and serving a congregation that certainly included both free and enslaved people of African descent. Following Emancipation, the church became a spiritual home for newly freed Jamaicans seeking baptisms and marriages. It developed into a full parish on Tower Street, but its early history was catastrophically interrupted on January 14, 1907, when the great Kingston earthquake completely leveled the building. Under the leadership of Reverend Stephen Oliver Ormsby, the congregation demonstrated remarkable resilience, eventually rebuilding and establishing the Ormsby Memorial Hall in 1930, ensuring the church's continued presence for future generations.",
