    "status": "active",
    "classification": "Parish Church (Church)",
    "intro": "St. Mark's Anglican Parish Church is the principal Anglican church of Mandeville, the capital of Manchester parish and the main urban centre of Jamaica's central highlands. Confirmed at its Mandeville location, the church is described by reviewers as consecrated in 1820 in Gothic style, built from stone, with 'stunning architecture — stone walls, pointed arch doors and windows.' It is described as a 'Mandeville historical treasure' and is 'centrally located — easy to access,' a 'solid refuge welcoming the lost, the faithful, the hopeful' that has withstood the test of time.",
    "history": "Mandeville was established as the capital of Manchester parish in 1816 when Manchester was carved out of the older parishes. St. Mark's Parish Church was consecrated in 1820, just four years after the town's founding, and has been the religious and civic heart of Mandeville ever since. The church was built in the Gothic revival style that was fashionable in the early 19th century, with local stone giving it a distinctive appearance and durability. Mandeville is the highest-elevation parish capital in Jamaica, set at approximately 600 metres above sea level, and its cool climate attracted a substantial European and later North American residential community.",
    "architecture": "St. Mark's Parish Church is confirmed at coordinates 18.0375, -77.5080 in central Mandeville. Reviewers describe it as a beautiful Gothic-style stone church consecrated in 1820, with pointed arch doors and windows and cool stone walls. The church is centrally located in Mandeville with adequate parking. One reviewer describes leaning against its cool stone walls in the heat of day — though at Mandeville's elevation, the temperature is considerably milder than the coast. The elevated interior position protects it from coastal surge while creating wind exposure during major storm passages.",
    "clergy": "St. Mark's Parish Church has been the seat of the Manchester deanery and one of the most important Anglican churches in central Jamaica. As the principal church of the parish capital, its clergy have served as leaders of the Manchester circuit and prominent figures in Jamaican Anglican life.",
    "interestingFacts": "Mandeville's status as the highest parish capital in Jamaica has given it a distinct character — the town attracted British and later North American retirees and residents drawn by its cool climate, and it has a more 'English' feel than most Jamaican towns. The church, consecrated only four years after the town's founding in 1816, has been at the centre of Mandeville's community life for over two centuries. St. Mark's Day (25 April) falls in the spring — a time of year when Mandeville's weather is at its most pleasant.",
