    "clergy": "Historically, August Town was served by clergy from larger parishes like St. Andrew's in Half Way Tree, who traveled to the mission for services. Today, St. Cyprian’s is part of a modern clergy cluster with St. Margaret’s in Liguanea and St. Joseph’s in The Grove, sharing pastoral resources. The day-to-day ministry relies heavily on dedicated lay leaders—wardens, choir members, and Mothers’ Union members—who sustain worship, pastoral care, and community ties. The church also connects with local institutions like the August Town Primary School, which serves as a community hub and emergency shelter.",
    "interestingFacts": "August Town holds a significant place in Jamaican religious history as the center of Alexander Bedward's movement, with his tomb designated a National Heritage Site. This context makes the enduring presence of St. Cyprian’s a testament to the diverse spiritual landscape of the community. During Hurricane Melissa (2025), while the church building was not reported as destroyed, the community suffered greatly, with many residents losing roofs and walls. The church and its networks became critical support centers, helping to organize meals and relief efforts for affected families, continuing its long-standing role of standing with the community through times of crisis.",
