    "intro": "St. Luke’s Anglican Church serves the small rural district of Aboukir in central St. Ann. It was established in the late 1800s for the hillside communities formed by freed families after Emancipation in 1838. Despite facing storms and other challenges over the years, the church has remained a vital place of worship. Today, it continues to serve Aboukir as a steadfast symbol of both faith and community history.",
    "history": "St. Luke's Anglican Church was founded in the late 19th century, as freed people established new villages in the hills of the Aboukir district after Emancipation. The area was previously part of a landscape dominated by plantations near Alexandria and Coleyville, where the population was enslaved. As these new communities grew, the Anglican Diocese began sending clergy from Alexandria and Brown's Town to hold services. By the 1880s, a permanent stone chapel was completed and dedicated to St. Luke, the biblical evangelist known for compassion and healing. The church quickly became a central hub for baptisms, education, and community gatherings during Jamaica's transition to a free society.",
