    "clergy": "St. Luke’s has always been part of the St. Ann Deanery, sharing clergy with neighboring missions in Alexandria, Madras, and Brittonville. Early priests traveled difficult hillside roads to lead worship and support local education and family life. The church was instrumental in providing basic literacy and religious instruction, which were especially vital in the post-Emancipation era. After Jamaica’s Independence in 1962, the congregation remained active through the Mothers’ Union, youth programs, and community events. Lay readers were essential in maintaining regular services when ordained clergy were serving multiple congregations.",
    "interestingFacts": "St. Luke’s has endured several major hurricanes, including Charlie (1951) and Gilbert (1988), which caused damage to the roof and windows. Each time, the community, with support from the Diocese, carried out the necessary repairs. The sturdy stone structure has also withstood occasional flooding from heavy hillside rains. There are no reports of damage from Hurricane Melissa (2025). Today, St. Luke’s Anglican Church remains an active parish, holding weekly services, weddings, and funerals, standing as a living reminder of Aboukir’s deep community heritage and enduring faith.",
