    "classification": "Chapel/Church",
    "intro": "St. John's Anglican Church at Sligoville holds a particular historical significance in Jamaica: Sligoville was the first free village established in Jamaica after Emancipation in 1838, founded on land provided by the Marquess of Sligo, then Governor of Jamaica. The church confirmed at its Sligoville Road location is described by a reviewer as 'a place of historic significance' — with a caution to watch for wasps in the eaves, bell tower, and around the tombstones. This is a church rooted in the earliest chapter of Jamaican freedom.",
    "history": "Sligoville was established in 1838 as the first free village in Jamaica, created when Howe Peter Browne, 2nd Marquess of Sligo and Governor of Jamaica, provided land in the hills of St. Catherine for freed people to settle on their own terms. The village became a symbol of post-Emancipation aspiration — a demonstration that freed Jamaicans could organise a settled, self-supporting community outside the plantation system. The Anglican church at Sligoville served this pioneering free community, which was also home to Baptist and other nonconformist congregations. The name Sligoville directly commemorates the Marquess whose support made the settlement possible.",
    "architecture": "St. John's is confirmed at coordinates 18.0876, -76.9419 on the Sligoville Road. The church sits in the hill country of St. Catherine, at elevation above the coastal plain. The reviewer's note about wasps in the eaves and bell tower, and around the tombstones, paints a picture of a historic building with an old churchyard in a quiet hill setting. The church's age means it has stood through the full history of post-Emancipation Jamaica, from the first days of freedom to the present.",
    "clergy": "Sligoville has been served within the St. Catherine deanery. The church's connection to the first free village gives it a particular pastoral identity — a congregation whose origins lie in the most hopeful moment of Jamaican social history.",
    "interestingFacts": "Sligoville's status as Jamaica's first free village is one of the most significant facts in the social history of the post-Emancipation Caribbean. The Marquess of Sligo was an unusually progressive colonial governor who used his personal resources and position to facilitate the creation of the village. Sligoville became a model for subsequent free village development across Jamaica and is commemorated as a site of national heritage significance. The church here has served the descendants of Jamaica's first free community for nearly two centuries.",
