    "clergy": "St. Thomas has always been a key church in the Black River Deanery. It was served by many well-known priests, some of whom also ministered in nearby villages like Mayfield and Middle Quarters. The church once ran its own school, teaching local children reading, writing, and religion. After Jamaica’s Independence in 1962, St. Thomas remained active with youth programs, Mothers’ Union activities, and community outreach. Lay readers (trained volunteers) were essential in leading services when a full-time priest was shared with other churches.",
    "interestingFacts": "St. Thomas has endured many natural disasters, including Hurricane Charlie (1951), Hurricane Gilbert (1988), and floods from the nearby Black River. The church has gone through periods of decline, but each time, the community has worked hard to repair and reopen it. In recent years, small restoration projects have kept the roof, windows, and walls in good condition. The church has a sturdy construction and benefitted from ongoing maintenance. Today, St. Thomas Anglican Church continues to hold regular services, weddings, funerals, and community events, standing as a enduring symbol of faith and history in Lacovia. Hurricane Melissa (October 28, 2025) decimated the roof of St Thomas Anglican Church in Lacovia. The Diocese confirmed it as one of four Anglican churches that 'stood little chance' against the hurricane. The Gleaner (January 2026) reported the roof was 'decimated' with a photograph showing the exposed interior. The Social Development Commission's post-Melissa church restoration programme received applications including churches from the Lacovia area, underscoring the widespread damage. As of early 2026 the church required major roof reconstruction.",
