    "architecture": "All Saints is a strong limestone church built in the Victorian Gothic style. It has a tall west tower with a belfry, a long rectangular nave (where the congregation sits), and a small chancel at the eastern end. The steep roof, once covered with wooden shingles and now with metal, fits well with the rainy mountain climate. Inside, the church has an open wooden roof, smooth stone floors, and wooden pews. Large pointed windows with colored glass brighten the space, and the churchyard holds many old graves of teachers, clergy, and residents from the 1800s onward.",
    "clergy": "The church has always been part of the Black River Deanery and was served by many well-known Anglican priests, some of whom also worked in the nearby schools. Clergy like Canon W. R. Campbell played important roles in both education and worship. Students from Hampton and Munro attended services here, and school events were often held in the church. After Jamaica gained Independence in 1962, the church continued to support youth programs, choirs, and Mothers’ Union activities. Lay readers helped keep the church active during times when clergy were shared across the parish.",
    "interestingFacts": "All Saints has survived many natural disasters. Hurricanes Charlie (1951) and Gilbert (1988) damaged the roof and tower, but repairs were made by parishioners and the Diocese. The church also came through Hurricane Melissa (2025) without any major reported damage. Today, it remains well maintained and continues to host school services, weddings, funerals, and community events. All Saints Anglican Church stands as a strong symbol of faith, history, and education in the Malvern hills, connecting Jamaica’s past with its present.",
