    "intro": "St. Thomas' Parish Church is the principal Anglican church of Morant Bay, the capital of St. Thomas parish, situated at 9 Highbury Close with a panoramic view over the Caribbean Sea. It is one of the most historically significant Anglican churches in Jamaica, standing at the centre of a town whose name is inseparable from the 1865 Morant Bay Rebellion, the most consequential civil uprising in 19th-century Jamaican history. The church's commanding position overlooking the harbour gives it a presence that matches its historical weight within the story of the parish.",
    "history": "Morant Bay was the site of the Morant Bay Rebellion of October 1865, led by Paul Bogle, a deacon of the Native Baptist Church, alongside George William Gordon, a mixed-race politician and justice of the peace. The uprising began when a crowd of protesters marched on the Morant Bay courthouse, leading to a violent confrontation that left 25 people dead. The British colonial government's suppression was brutal: over 430 people were executed, including Paul Bogle and George William Gordon, and more than 1,000 homes were burned. Both Bogle and Gordon are now National Heroes of Jamaica. The Anglican parish church stands near this courthouse, and the history of Anglican clergy in Morant Bay is complexly entangled with the colonial response to the rebellion. The church itself predates the rebellion and has served the parish capital across more than two centuries.",
