    "interestingFacts": "St. Michael’s has endured several major hurricanes, including Hurricane Charlie (1951) and Hurricane Gilbert (1988), which caused damage to the roof and windows. Each time, the congregation, with support from the Diocese, carried out the necessary repairs. Further restoration work in more recent years has helped preserve the building. There are no reports of damage from Today, St. Michael’s Anglican Church remains active, holding weekly services, weddings, funerals, and harvest festivals, continuing its role as a stable symbol of faith and community in Clarks Town. Hurricane Melissa (October 28, 2025) tore away large sections of the roof at St Michael's Anglican Church in Clark's Town, causing severe structural damage. The Jamaica Gleaner reported the damage with a photograph showing the exposed interior and torn roof sections. The Gleaner's 'past in ruins' report (November 2025) listed St Michael's Clark's Town among dozens of heritage structures damaged across the western parishes. Clark's Town is in the Trelawny interior, which the Diocese's Claudette Marshall identified as one of the three worst-affected deaneries alongside St Elizabeth and Westmoreland. As of late 2025, the church required major roof reconstruction and structural assessment.",
